   - Battery costs fell to $92/kWh in 2026, driven by lithium iron phosphate (LFP) dominance (58% of mass-market cells) and factory utilization rates of 81%.
   - Storage is reshaping renewable systems: batteries enable solar-to-grid flexibility, shape electricity prices, and provide firm delivery for contracted projects.
   - Recycling and second-life applications are emerging as strategic feedstocks, though economic viability depends on chemistry (e.g., higher-value nickel-rich batteries vs. LFP logistics).

**Key Insight**: The renewable energy transition is shifting from deployment volume to system value. Success now depends on integrating storage, flexible demand, and grid upgrades—rather than just low-cost modules or turbines. Markets that coordinate these elements will capture more of the declining cost curve, while those neglecting integration face curtailment and weaker returns.
